# SUMMARY
This file describes how to build complex Shapes out of several parts

# KEY CONCEPTS
- Placement is always based on an Axis-Aligned Bounding Box
- we can refer to West, East, South, North, Top and Bottom Sides
- Shapes have a Place() method with several helper methods for placing shapes relative to eachother

# BASIC SHAPES
- Box - `GeometryBuilder\Shapes\SimpleShapes\Box.cs' 
	- basic cuboid
- Cylinder - `GeometryBuilder\Shapes\SimpleShapes\Cylinder.cs' 
	- basic cylinder
- ComplexShapePart - `GeometryBuilder\Shapes\ComplexShapePart.cs`
	- this acts like a box, until it is given children
	- think of these like a placeholder for the next level of detail

# ADDING CHILD SHAPES
- always add a shape as a child of this project. 
	- The "AddChild" method returns the added object, so you can create the object and make it a child in the same line
	- never just create a shape. Always wrap a new shape in "AddChild" so it is automatically added to the parent.

# MEASUREMENTS
- use Measure.Feet for larger distances and Measure.Inches for smaller
	- avoid using raw distances
- place all measurements as readonly class fields and place them at the top of the class
	- avoid hard-coding any measurments

# SIDE-BASED PLACEMENT
- when a shape has a wall side, use side.GetAxis() to get the axis that moves into or out of that wall
- use side.GetPerpendicularAxis() to get the axis that moves left and right along the wall

# SIZING AND POSITIONING
- there are numerous ways to size and position shapes, each has its own benefits. Prefer the approaches in this order,
most to least.
1. using Place()
	- this assumes you have set the object size already
	- this lets you place an object on a side of its parent (inner or outer)
2. using Adjust()
	- you can start by matching another object's size and position with From()
	- then you can create "slices" from each axis
	- you can also add, or set, any of the axis, which is useful for stretching and shrinking
3. directly setting Sides
	- any side can be set as anchored (default) or unanchored
	- when "anchored", the whole shape moves as one
	- when "unanchored", you move the side, but the rest of the shape stays where it is, thus changing the size
4. directly setting Position and Size

## How to choose the best placement method
- use "Place()" if you know the size of the object
- use "Adjust()" with Slicing if this shape is a subset of the parent bounding box, for example, a left side